.nh
.TH "SHA384SUMX" "1" "Jan 2024" "gnux" "gnux manual"

.SH NAME
sha384sumx - Print or check SHA-384 (384-bit) checksums.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsha384sumx [OPTION]... [FILE]...\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
sha384sumx prints or checks SHA-384 message digests (FIPS 180-4, 384 bits). For each FILE it prints one line: the digest in hexadecimal, a space, a mode mark (a space for text mode, '*' with -b), and the file name. With no FILE, or when FILE is -, it reads standard input. Files are always read as bytes, so -b and -t change only the mark; when both are given, -t wins. --tag prints BSD-style lines instead ('ALGORITHM (FILE) = DIGEST'). A file name containing a backslash, newline or carriage return is escaped and its line starts with a backslash, unless -z ends lines with NUL instead. -c reads such lines (in either style) from the FILEs and checks each listed file, printing 'FILE: OK' or 'FILE: FAILED' and a summary warning of mismatches, unreadable files and improperly formatted lines; --quiet drops the OK lines, --status prints nothing, --ignore-missing skips listed files that do not exist, -w warns about each improperly formatted line and --strict fails because of them. The exit status is 0 when everything was read (and, with -c, matched), 1 otherwise. On Windows a backslash in a file name is the path separator, so it is not escaped as GNU escapes it on Unix; a name with a newline is still escaped.

.PP
EXIT STATUS.
  0  success: every operand was processed
  1  failure: an operand could not be processed (diagnosed on standard error), or a command-line mistake

.PP
HELP. sha384sumx --help shows the options and the first examples; gnux help sha384sumx prints the full manual, and gnux help sha384sumx OPTION explains one option.

.PP
SEE ALSO. cksumx, md5sumx, sha1sumx, sha256sumx, sha512sumx, b2sumx.

.PP
PERMISSIONS. Needs read permission on each FILE (and, with -c, on the checksum lists and every file they name) and search permission on the directories leading to them. Nothing is ever modified.

.PP
USE CASES.
  - Verifying a download against the checksum its publisher lists.
  - Recording digests of release artifacts so users can check them.
  - Detecting whether files changed between two points in time.
  - Finding duplicate files by comparing their digests.
  - Checking a backup or a copy against the original.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-b\fP, \fB--binary\fP[=false]
	read in binary mode

.PP
\fB-c\fP, \fB--check\fP[=false]
	read checksums from the FILEs and check them

.PP
\fB--help\fP[=false]
	display this help and exit

.PP
\fB--ignore-missing\fP[=false]
	don't fail or report status for missing files

.PP
\fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	don't print OK for each successfully verified file

.PP
\fB--status\fP[=false]
	don't output anything, status code shows success

.PP
\fB--strict\fP[=false]
	exit non-zero for improperly formatted checksum lines

.PP
\fB--tag\fP[=false]
	create a BSD-style checksum

.PP
\fB-t\fP, \fB--text\fP[=false]
	read in text mode (default)

.PP
\fB--version\fP[=false]
	output version information and exit

.PP
\fB-w\fP, \fB--warn\fP[=false]
	warn about improperly formatted checksum lines

.PP
\fB-z\fP, \fB--zero\fP[=false]
	end each output line with NUL, not newline, and disable file name escaping


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # You downloaded an installer: print its digest to compare with the one on the website
  sha384sumx ubuntu-24.04-desktop-amd64.iso

  # Verify the download against the publisher's checksum file, which lists other files too
  sha384sumx -c --ignore-missing sha384.sums

  # Publish checksums next to your release archives
  sha384sumx dist/*.tar.gz > dist/sha384.sums

  # Check that a backup copied to a USB drive still matches the original
  sha384sumx photos.tar /media/usb/photos.tar

  # Find duplicate photos in a folder: files with the same digest have the same content
  sha384sumx *.jpg | sortx | uniqx -w 96 -D

  # In CI, fail the build when a vendored file was changed by hand
  sha384sumx -c --status vendor.sha384sums || { echox 'vendored files changed' >&2; exit 1; }

  # Re-check a large archive set and show only what no longer matches
  sha384sumx -c --quiet sha384.sums

  # Hash a token from a variable, without the newline echo would add
  printfx '%s' "$TOKEN" | sha384sumx

  # Write lines that name the algorithm, so a mixed list can be checked with cksumx -c
  sha384sumx --tag release.tar.gz >> CHECKSUMS

  # Refuse a checksum list with malformed lines instead of skipping them
  sha384sumx -c --strict -w sha384.sums

  # Match the '*' binary-mode lines produced by tools on Windows
  sha384sumx -b setup.exe

  # Record a digest next to the file in the portable gnux shell, the same on every OS
  gnux shell -c 'sha384sumx file.iso > file.iso.sha384sum'
.EE
